@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

: a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:about :localTermNamespaceConsistency .

:localTermNamespaceConsistency a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "Local ontology terms must be USED under their own namespace, not schema.org, at every call site"@en ;
    schema:description "A property or class correctly minted in a document's local ontology namespace (: prefix) is a gate violation the moment it is invoked anywhere with the schema: prefix instead — this fabricates a nonexistent schema.org term even though the local declaration itself was correct."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-07-30T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-07-30T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:step :step1, :step2, :step3 .

:step1 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    schema:name "How the violation happens"@en ;
    schema:text """Caught 2026-07-30 on ontologies-agentic-systems-latent-space-mesh-claude_sonnet_5-1.ttl: `:meshesWith` was correctly declared as `a rdf:Property` under the document's own local namespace (`: <https://www.latent.space/p/ontologies-agentic-systems#>`), with a proper `rdfs:domain`/`rdfs:range`/`rdfs:comment` and `rdfs:isDefinedBy :`. But the actual usage — the one place the property was asserted on `:article` — was written as `schema:meshesWith` instead of `:meshesWith`. This fabricates a nonexistent `http://schema.org/meshesWith` term at the point of use, even though the declaration itself, three lines up, was fully correct. The bug is a namespace-prefix slip at the call site, not a missing declaration — grepping only for 'is this term declared somewhere' would have missed it entirely, since it WAS declared, just under the wrong prefix when invoked."""@en .

:step2 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    schema:name "BLOCKING CHECK — cross-reference every locally-declared term's declaration prefix against its usage prefix"@en ;
    schema:text """Before delivering any generated TTL that mints its own lightweight ontology (custom classes/properties under the document's `: <...#>` namespace):

1. Extract every local term name declared with `a rdf:Property`, `a rdfs:Class`, `a owl:Class`, or similar, under the bare `:` prefix — e.g. `grep -oE '^:[A-Za-z]+ a (rdf:Property|rdfs:Class|owl:Class)' file.ttl`.
2. For each such term name (e.g. `meshesWith`), grep the WHOLE file for `schema:{termName}`, `dbr:{termName}`, or any other foreign-vocabulary prefix immediately followed by that exact identifier — e.g. `grep -n 'schema:meshesWith\\|dbr:meshesWith' file.ttl`. Any hit is a violation: the term was declared locally but invoked under a namespace that does not own it.
3. Fix by replacing the foreign-prefixed usage with the bare local form (`:meshesWith`), never the reverse — the local declaration is the source of truth since it is what makes the term legitimate in the first place.

This check is orthogonal to, and does not replace, the existing ontology cross-reference gate (reuse-via-bound-prefix for genuinely EXISTING canonical terms, Step 153) — that gate governs whether to mint a new term at all; this gate governs whether an already-correctly-minted term is invoked under its own namespace everywhere it appears."""@en .

:step3 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    schema:name "Applies to every foreign vocabulary, not just schema.org"@en ;
    schema:text """The same slip can happen against any prefix in scope — a locally-minted class invoked as `dbr:{LocalClassName}`, `owl:{LocalPropertyName}`, or any other declared prefix that happens to be in the same file. The check in Step 2 must be run against every non-local prefix declared in the file's `@prefix` block, not schema: alone, since the failure mode (correct declaration, wrong-prefix invocation) is prefix-agnostic."""@en .
